[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Primary Constructors in C# 9.0 and Later"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"C# 9.0 introduced primary constructors, a concise syntax for declaring class constructors and initializing properties directly in the class declaration. This feature streamlines code and reduces boilerplate, making classes more readable and maintainable. This feature was further enhanced in later versions of C#."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Syntax"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The primary constructor parameters are declared within the parentheses following the class name. These parameters are then available throughout the class scope for initializing properties or fields."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public class Person(string firstName, string lastName)
{
    public string FirstName { get; } = firstName;
    public string LastName { get; } = lastName;

    public string FullName => $"{FirstName} {LastName}";
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'firstName' and 'lastName' are primary constructor parameters. They are used to initialize the 'FirstName' and 'LastName' properties. The 'FullName' property demonstrates how these parameters can be used within the class."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Primary Constructors"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced Boilerplate: Eliminates the need to write explicit constructor declarations for simple initialization scenarios."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved Readability: Makes class declarations more concise and easier to understand."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enhanced Maintainability: Simplifies code changes and reduces the risk of errors."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Primary Constructors with Fields"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Primary constructor parameters can also be used to initialize fields. This is useful when you need to store the parameter values for internal use within the class."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public class Circle(double radius)
{
    private readonly double _radius = radius;

    public double Area => Math.PI * _radius * _radius;

    public double Circumference => 2 * Math.PI * _radius;
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, the 'radius' parameter is used to initialize the private field '_radius'. This field is then used to calculate the 'Area' and 'Circumference' properties."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Considerations and Limitations"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Accessibility: Primary constructor parameters are implicitly private. If you need to expose them, you must explicitly declare properties or fields."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Complex Initialization: For more complex initialization logic, you might still need to use a traditional constructor."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"C# Version: Primary constructors are available in C# 9.0 and later versions. Ensure your project is configured to use a compatible language version."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Example with Dependency Injection"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Primary constructors are particularly useful in scenarios involving dependency injection. They allow you to easily inject dependencies into your classes."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"public interface ILogger
{
    void Log(string message);
}

public class MyService(ILogger logger)
{
    public void DoSomething()
    {
        logger.Log("Doing something...");
    }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'ILogger' is injected into 'MyService' using the primary constructor. This makes it easy to test and configure 'MyService' with different logger implementations."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Primary constructors provide a powerful and concise way to declare constructors and initialize properties in C#. By reducing boilerplate and improving readability, they can significantly enhance the development experience. Understanding and utilizing primary constructors can lead to cleaner, more maintainable code."}]
